Steam Enhanced, client/navigation/nav-rail.css: the title bar's button wrappers picked by their class.

Why. The rail places Steam's title bar buttons (Gamepad toggle, Announcements, Notifications,
account) by the wrapper Steam puts around each one, `TitleBarControls > :has(<button class>)`. That
compound is only a :has(): Blink files such a rule with the universal ones and evaluates the :has()
on the elements it is tried on, marking them as :has() anchors, and every DOM change under the
title bar then re-checks them. With the five wrappers position: fixed, each relayout of the main
window cost about twice as much. Measured in the main window, window occluded, so its frames driven over CDP
(a forced style and layout every 16 ms, ResizeObserver delivered after it), Home -> CS2's game page, 3 s after the
route change (Performance.getMetrics, mean of 2-3 runs): layout 560 ms in 27 passes (~21 ms each)
-> 297 ms (~11 ms each); the same 27 passes, the same dirty objects. Removing these selectors
outright gave the same 281 ms.

What. Each of those :has() gets the wrapper's class: `:where(.tool-tip-source):has(...)` (Steam's
tooltip source; every wrapper there is one). :where() keeps every selector's weight as it was: the
offsets below rely on it (one :is() per button for that reason). Computed styles of every element
on the game page and on Home are identical before and after (DOMSnapshot, all properties).

